WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.952
This lesson is one of the heaviest in M8 because it combines a boundary condition, an angle threshold, and a special optical outcome.

2
00:00:04.952 --> 00:00:09.903
Total internal reflection does not happen just because the angle is large.

3
00:00:09.903 --> 00:00:14.855
It only happens when light is trying to leave the more optically dense medium for the less dense one.

4
00:00:14.855 --> 00:00:19.806
At one special angle, the refracted route skims along the boundary.

5
00:00:19.806 --> 00:00:24.758
That is the critical angle.

6
00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:29.709
Above that limit, there is no refracted escape route at all.

7
00:00:29.709 --> 00:00:34.661
The light is reflected back inside.

8
00:00:34.661 --> 00:00:39.612
When those pieces are kept together, the lesson becomes surprisingly logical.

9
00:00:39.612 --> 00:00:44.564
Keep this takeaway in view: Total internal reflection only makes sense when medium direction, limit angle, and escape behavior are kept together.

10
00:00:44.564 --> 00:00:49.515
Direction condition means denser to less dense.

11
00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:54.467
The route must be trying to leave the optically denser medium.

12
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:59.418
Limit case means critical angle.

13
00:00:59.418 --> 00:01:04.370
At this angle the refracted route skims along the boundary.

14
00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:10.291
This diagram is powerful because it does not treat the critical angle as a floating definition.

15
00:01:10.291 --> 00:01:16.212
It shows the full sequence.

16
00:01:16.212 --> 00:01:22.133
Below the critical angle, there is still a refracted escape route.

17
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:28.054
At the critical angle, the refracted route lies along the boundary at ninety degrees to the Guide Line.

18
00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:33.976
Above the critical angle, the refracted route disappears and the light reflects back inside the denser medium.

19
00:01:33.976 --> 00:01:39.897
Reading those three cases as one continuous story makes the threshold much easier to trust.

20
00:01:39.897 --> 00:01:45.818
Keep this takeaway in view: The critical angle becomes meaningful when it is read as the last escape case in a sequence.

21
00:01:45.818 --> 00:01:51.739
Below limit: There is still an escaping refracted route.

22
00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:57.660
At limit: The refracted route skims along the surface.

23
00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.677
Think of a runner approaching the edge of a track.

24
00:02:01.677 --> 00:02:05.694
At smaller steering angles, the runner can still exit.

25
00:02:05.694 --> 00:02:09.711
At one exact angle, the runner just skims along the boundary.

26
00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:13.729
Beyond that, the runner is forced to stay inside and rebound along the track.

27
00:02:13.729 --> 00:02:17.746
The analogy is not about mechanics.

28
00:02:17.746 --> 00:02:21.763
It is about threshold behavior.

29
00:02:21.763 --> 00:02:25.780
Total internal reflection is not a vague change.

30
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.797
It is a clear limit case followed by a new regime.

31
00:02:29.797 --> 00:02:33.814
That is why the lesson becomes powerful once the critical angle is seen as an edge condition instead of as one more equation to memorize.

32
00:02:33.814 --> 00:02:37.831
Keep this takeaway in view: The critical angle is best understood as the edge between escape and trapped reflection.

33
00:02:37.831 --> 00:02:41.849
Escape stage means below limit.

34
00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:45.866
The route can still leave the original region.

35
00:02:45.866 --> 00:02:49.883
Edge stage means just skimming.

36
00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:53.900
This matches the critical-angle case.

37
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:59.490
The conceptual rule comes first.

38
00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:05.080
The critical angle is the angle of incidence in the denser medium that gives a ninety-degree refracted route in the less dense medium.

39
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:10.670
The total-internal-reflection rule comes second.

40
00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:16.260
It happens only when the incident angle is greater than the critical angle and the light is travelling from the denser medium toward the less dense one.

41
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.850
A useful numerical link for denser-to-air work is sine of c equals one over n, where n is the refractive index of the denser medium.

42
00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:27.440
But even when the lesson becomes numerical, the escape-limit picture should stay in charge.

43
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.030
Keep this takeaway in view: Critical-angle work becomes safer when the angle threshold and the medium direction are checked together.

44
00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:38.620
Direction condition: Light must be leaving the optically denser medium.

45
00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:44.210
Critical case: The refracted route lies along the boundary at the threshold.

46
00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:49.800
critical angle -> refracted route at 90 degrees to the Guide Line.

47
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:55.390
total internal reflection happens for angle > critical angle.

48
00:03:55.390 --> 00:04:00.980
The angle condition is not enough by itself.

49
00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:06.570
The medium direction must also be correct.

50
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:12.432
A beautiful pattern in this lesson is that one boundary rule scales up into a technology.

51
00:04:12.432 --> 00:04:18.294
In an optical fiber, light repeatedly meets the boundary between the denser fiber core and the less dense surrounding material.

52
00:04:18.294 --> 00:04:24.155
If the incidence condition stays above the critical angle, the light keeps reflecting internally and stays guided through the fiber.

53
00:04:24.155 --> 00:04:30.017
This is not a different rule.

54
00:04:30.017 --> 00:04:35.879
It is repeated total internal reflection.

55
00:04:35.879 --> 00:04:41.741
Seeing that connection helps students remember that the lesson is about controlled escape versus trapped guidance, not about memorizing isolated definitions.

56
00:04:41.741 --> 00:04:47.603
Keep this takeaway in view: Optical fibers are a repeated total-internal-reflection story built from the same boundary rule.

57
00:04:47.603 --> 00:04:53.465
Single boundary means one threshold event.

58
00:04:53.465 --> 00:04:59.326
Compare below, at, and above the critical angle.

59
00:04:59.326 --> 00:05:05.188
Repeated boundary means fiber guidance.

60
00:05:05.188 --> 00:05:11.050
Repeated internal reflections keep light inside the core.

61
00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.641
Try five checks.

62
00:05:14.641 --> 00:05:18.232
What is the critical angle?

63
00:05:18.232 --> 00:05:21.824
The last incidence angle in the denser medium that still gives a refracted route along the boundary.

64
00:05:21.824 --> 00:05:25.415
What happens below it?

65
00:05:25.415 --> 00:05:29.006
Light still escapes.

66
00:05:29.006 --> 00:05:32.597
What happens above it?

67
00:05:32.597 --> 00:05:36.188
There is total internal reflection.

68
00:05:36.188 --> 00:05:39.779
Is a large angle alone enough for total internal reflection?

69
00:05:39.779 --> 00:05:43.371
No, the light also has to be travelling from the denser medium toward the less dense one.

70
00:05:43.371 --> 00:05:46.962
And what technology uses repeated internal reflection?

71
00:05:46.962 --> 00:05:50.553
Optical fibers.

72
00:05:50.553 --> 00:05:54.144
Those checks cover almost the whole lesson.

73
00:05:54.144 --> 00:05:57.735
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the critical-angle lesson now reads like one continuous boundary story.

74
00:05:57.735 --> 00:06:01.326
Definition means last escape angle.

75
00:06:01.326 --> 00:06:04.918
That is the most reliable plain-language description of the critical angle.

76
00:06:04.918 --> 00:06:08.509
Below c means escape remains.

77
00:06:08.509 --> 00:06:12.100
There is still a refracted route out of the denser medium.

78
00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:18.448
You are ready for the worked example now.

79
00:06:18.448 --> 00:06:24.795
Start by identifying which side is optically denser and which way the light is travelling.

80
00:06:24.795 --> 00:06:31.143
Then ask whether the incidence angle is below, equal to, or above the critical angle.

81
00:06:31.143 --> 00:06:37.490
If it is below, expect escape refraction.

82
00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:43.838
If it is equal, expect a boundary-skimming refracted route.

83
00:06:43.838 --> 00:06:50.185
If it is above and the medium direction is correct, expect total internal reflection.

84
00:06:50.185 --> 00:06:56.532
If the problem gives refractive index, use the critical-angle equation only after the boundary story is already clear.

85
00:06:56.532 --> 00:07:02.880
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: critical-angle reasoning now has a clear threshold story and a strong application link.

86
00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:09.228
Step 1: Identify the denser and less dense media before looking at the angle.

87
00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:15.575
Step 2: Compare the incidence angle with the critical angle.

88
00:07:15.575 --> 00:07:21.922
check whether the light is leaving the denser medium.

89
00:07:21.922 --> 00:07:28.270
The strongest total-internal-reflection answers treat the critical angle as part of a boundary sequence rather than as an isolated definition.
